Under internal policy [POL-PRIV-2026-V2 / Sec. 301.1], Under 15 U.S.C. 1681b, the bank may obtain and use a consumer credit report ONLY when a documented permissible purpose exists, strictly limited to:
- In accordance with the written instructions of the consumer to whom it relates;
- In connection with a credit transaction involving the consumer on which the consumer has applied or for review or collection of an account;
- For employment purposes (only with clear, standalone written authorization from the consumer);
- In connection with the determination of the consumer's eligibility for a license or benefit granted by a governmental instrumentality.

Under internal policy [POL-LEND-2026-V3 / Sec. 202.1], A creditor shall not discriminate against any applicant on a prohibited basis regarding any aspect of a credit transaction. Prohibited bases include:
- Race, color, religion, national origin, sex, marital status, or age (provided the applicant has the capacity to contract);
- The fact that all or part of the applicant's income derives from any public assistance program;
- The fact that the applicant has in good faith exercised any right under the Consumer Credit Protection Act.

Under internal policy [POL-LEND-2026-V3 / Sec. 202.2], Within thirty (30) calendar days after receiving a completed credit application, the bank must notify the applicant of its approval, counteroffer, or adverse action (denial). If adverse action is taken, the notice must:
- Be in writing;
- Disclose the specific reasons for denial (or inform the applicant of their right to receive the reasons within 60 days upon written request);
- Provide the required ECOA notice of non-discrimination and supervisory agency contact details.
